{"study_accession":"PRJDB18080","secondary_study_accession":"DRP013829","title":"In vitro induction of antigen-specific and functionally stable Treg cells from effector/memory conventional T cells for adoptive cell therapy of immunological diseases","description":"One strategy for antigen-specific immunosuppression is to convert antigen-specific conventional T cells (Tconvs) into Foxp3+ regulatory T cells (Tregs) as stably suppressive as naturally occurring Tregs (nTregs). To achieve the conversion in vitro in mice and humans, we induced high Foxp3 expression in antigen- and IL-2-stimulated Tconvs by CDK8/19 inhibition, and installed Treg-specific epigenetic changes in Treg signature genes, especially Foxp3, by depriving CD28 co-stimulation during the Treg induction. Repeating this process, with intermittent resting cultures solely with IL-2, enabled efficient conversion of naive as well as effector/memory CD4+ Tconvs, including Th1, Th2 and Th17 cells, into Foxp3+ Tregs transcriptionally and epigenetically similar to nTregs. Tregs thus induced were indeed functionally and phenotypically stable in vivo and effectively suppressed inflammatory bowel disease and graft-versus-host disease in mouse models. Adoptive cell therapy with such effector/memory Tconv-derived, functionally stable, induced Tregs can be instrumental in antigen- and disease-specific treatment of immunological diseases.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":8,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMD00901992","study_accession":"PRJDB18080","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"HR3_RNAseq_01_act_Tconv_N1","description":"HR3_RNAseq_01_act_Tconv_N1","sex":"","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-09-07","attributes":[["collection_date","2023"],["INSDC secondary accession","DRS500758"],["NCBI submission package","Generic.1.0"],["organism","Homo sapiens"],["geo_loc_name","Japan"],["tissue","Blood"],["sample_name","HR3_RNAseq_01_act_Tconv_N1"],["BioSam